 \ReviewerComment{1. Please report the exact training, validation, and testing periods and their respective sample sizes.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} already reports the partition sizes and forecast-target periods. We clarify the distinction between raw timestamps and usable target samples below. All times are UTC.
+
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Training.} Raw period: 1 January 2004 00:00--7 August 2016 06:00 (18,410 timestamps). Forecast-target period: 2 January 2004 12:00--7 August 2016 06:00 (18,404 targets).
+\item \textbf{Validation.} Raw period: 7 August 2016 12:00--20 April 2019 12:00 (3,945 timestamps). Forecast-target period: 9 August 2016 00:00--20 April 2019 12:00 (3,939 targets).
+\item \textbf{Testing.} Raw period: 20 April 2019 18:00--31 December 2021 18:00 (3,945 timestamps). Forecast-target period: 22 April 2019 06:00--31 December 2021 18:00 (3,939 targets).
+\end{itemize}
+
+The chronological split is made before window construction. Within each partition, six consecutive snapshots sampled every six hours form the input, and the next six-hour step is the forecast target. The first six timestamps in each partition therefore cannot serve as targets with a complete input window drawn from that partition; they are not six randomly missing observations. No window crosses a training, validation or testing boundary. The forecast lead is six hours from the last input snapshot, and the target is precipitation during the final hour of that lead, not a six-hour accumulation.
+
+These dates and counts refer to the main 70\%/15\%/15\% experiment, not the separate retrospective expanding-window evaluation addressed under R2-M5 and described in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_methods}. That evaluation has its own fold-specific periods and sample sizes. The existing manuscript already states the relevant main-experiment definitions; no new experiment, table or manuscript paragraph was needed for this response.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Existing Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset}: partition sizes, target-time intervals and raw training interval; its predictor/target-time paragraph defines the target hour. The separate rolling-origin protocol is in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_methods}. Manuscript and Supplementary text remain unchanged. Final page and line references pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing partition sizes and target-time ranges (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The main 2004--2021 experiment contains 18,410/3,945/3,945 raw training/validation/test timestamps and 18,404/3,939/3,939 forecast targets after six-snapshot windows are constructed separately within each partition. The target-time ranges (UTC) are 2 January 2004 12:00--7 August 2016 06:00 for training, 9 August 2016 00:00--20 April 2019 12:00 for validation, and 22 April 2019 06:00--31 December 2021 18:00 for testing. These are forecast target times, not input-window start times.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing raw training interval (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Feature screening used the absolute contemporaneous Spearman rank correlation with total precipitation, calculated over the 18,410 raw training-period timestamps from 00:00~UTC on 1 January 2004 to 06:00~UTC on 7 August 2016. These timestamps precede window construction and are distinct from the 18,404 training windows.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing forecast-origin and target-hour distinction (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Thus, the latest precipitation input represents $(t_0-1\,\mathrm{h},t_0]$, whereas the target represents $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$; no target-hour precipitation value is directly included in the input window.}
+}
 
